{"subject":"Pathology","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Pathology::85.Previous_Year_Questions","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532708","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":1,"stemHtml":"A smoker presents with an oral lesion, as shown in the image below. Which stain is used to identify this pathology?<br/><img src=\"images/image_101_163_1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supravital stain"},{"label":"B","text":"Congo red"},{"label":"C","text":"Hematoxylin and eosin (H&E)"},{"label":"D","text":"Oil red O"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Supravital stain","solutionHtml":"<p><strong>Correct Option A - <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8677c6e8251692777199817455a36f\">Supravital</span> stain:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The given image shows a laryngeal mass, for which <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"87f88d49311692777200eb848ea567\">toluidine</span> blue is used for identification, and it is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8677c6e8251692777199817455a36f\">supravital</span> stain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Options B, C, and D</strong> are not used to identify laryngeal mass.</p>\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532710","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":2,"stem":"Which among the following is the least radiosensitive cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"Platelets"},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocytes"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutrophils"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Platelets","solutionHtml":"<p>Among the given options, <strong>platelets</strong> are the <strong>least <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ff1a8ff3f16927771971636564c4a\">radiosensitive</span> cells.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Radiosensitivity</strong> refers to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6ced8252211692777199ab0cc3f2bb\">susceptibility</span> of cells or tissues to the effects of radiation.</li>\n<li>Cells with higher rates of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"8eedb6cec61692777196a6a3b93d3f\">proliferation</span> and less <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"55a682d72e16927771865bb4855ba3\">differentiation</span> tend to be more <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ff1a8ff3f16927771971636564c4a\">radiosensitive</span> because they are actively dividing and thus more vulnerable to the damaging effects of radiation.</li>\n<li>Platelets are the least <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3ff1a8ff3f16927771971636564c4a\">radiosensitive</span> as they do not divide actively.</li>\n<li>Order of Radiosensitivity:\n\t<ul>\n<li>Lymphocytes &amp;gt; neutrophils ~ monocytes &amp;gt; platelets</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Radiation sensitivity of human and murine peripheral blood lymphocytes, stem and progenitor cells - ScienceDirect.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532712","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":3,"stem":"Which of the following instruments is not used in the colorimetric method for the estimation of hemoglobin?","options":[],"answer":"","answerText":"","solutionHtml":"<p><strong>Correct Option: D)</strong></p>\n<p>The instrument shown in the image is an <strong>RBC pipette</strong> and it is not used in the colorimetric method for the estimation of hemoglobin, it is designed for <strong>manual blood cell counting.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p>The images in Options A, B, and C show a comparator, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ad2073fe6716927771885f3c1ec652\">hemoglobin</span> tube, and a stirrer respectively. They are components of Sahli's <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"648464850316927771888db6b676ae\">hemoglobinometer</span> set. Sahli's method of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ad2073fe6716927771885f3c1ec652\">hemoglobin</span> estimation is a colorimetric method.</p>\n<p><strong>Parts of </strong><strong>Sahli’s <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"648464850316927771888db6b676ae\">hemoglobinometer</span> set:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Comparator</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin Tube</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin Pipette</li>\n<li>Stirrer</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"444\" src=\"images/exp_105_78_1.jpg\" width=\"500\"/><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"202\" src=\"images/exp_105_78_2.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n<br/><img src=\"images/exp_105_78_1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_105_78_2.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532714","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":4,"stem":"Which of the following is not a part of the criteria for diagnosis of Kawasaki disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strawberry tongue"},{"label":"B","text":"Polymorphic rash"},{"label":"C","text":"Purulent conjunctivitis"},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical lymphadenopathy"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Purulent conjunctivitis","solutionHtml":"<p><strong>Correct Option C - <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0c02372a7d1692777196974ab1d772\">Purulent</span> conjunctivitis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"552e9ef8811692777186997018b858\">diagnostic</span> criteria for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"32342d80601692777190a5dd97a324\">Kawasaki disease</span> include non-purulent <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6ce77d5f5e1692777185cf7f8a67cd\">conjunctivitis</span> rather than <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0c02372a7d1692777196974ab1d772\">purulent</span> conjunctivitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Options A, B &amp; D: </strong>Strawberry tongue, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"61bad3c5dc1692777196997698f068\">Polymorphic</span> rash, and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"25c6ce766c1692777184ab9a40305c\">Cervical</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"344bcb4e391692777191307c1e0074\">lymphadenopathy</span> are all features of Kawasaki disease.</p>\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532716","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":5,"stem":"Which of the following disorders presents with repeated catalase-positive infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X linked hypogammaglobinemia"},{"label":"B","text":"SCID"},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic granulomatous disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Chediak Higashi Syndrome"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Chronic granulomatous disease","solutionHtml":"<p><strong>Correct Option C - Chronic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9e1f97f6ad169277718805c2dd35c1\">granulomatous</span> disease:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9e1f97f6ad169277718805c2dd35c1\">Granulomatous</span> Disease is characterized by a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"359a8718a61692777185568ac80abb\">defect</span> in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7c9e482521169277719313219f1bbe\">NADPH</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4a14dfa14e1692777194882f818d87\">oxidase</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0f160e4e42169277718771b715516a\">enzyme</span> complex. Without a functional <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7c9e482521169277719313219f1bbe\">NADPH</span> oxidase, the patient is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"55691242bb1692777199360f64cb67\">susceptible</span> to recurrent infections caused by catalase-positive organisms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Options A, B, and D</strong> are not associated with recurrent catalase-positive infections.</p>\n<br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">.</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532718","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":6,"stemHtml":"A Diabetic patient's blood sample is kept in the given container. What will be added to the blood sample?<br/><img src=\"images/pathology-l068-q031-p1521-stem-1.jpg\"/>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na fluoride"},{"label":"B","text":"EDTA"},{"label":"C","text":"Na citrate"},{"label":"D","text":"Heparin"}],"answer":"A","answerText":"Na fluoride","solutionHtml":"8f5\"&amp;gt;anticoagulant and <strong>sodium fluoride</strong> to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6b3de87b7c16927771901326efbcd2\">inhibit</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"185a500ee91692777188b3044db06d\">glycolysis</span> by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9dc12b322016927771830dff673eeb\">blocking</span> enolase.\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Colour</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Uses</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Whispyhistory,\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EDTA_(chelator_-_anticoagulant)_for_full_blood_count.jpg\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"129\" src=\"images/BnI2TKJUkOahGu7PMxVl1747225498.png\"\" width=\"400\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Purple/Lavender </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>EDTA (Ca2+ chelating anticoagulant)</p>\n<p><strong>(Option B ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>CBC, Peripheral Smear, ESR, HbA1c</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Gene Hobbs \" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partial_thromboplastin_time\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"325\" src=\"images/3VRj3LF1hTS34z6LvERe1747225498.png\"\" width=\"202\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Light Blue</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Trisodium Citrate (Ca2+ chelating anticoagulant)</p>\n<p><strong>(Option C ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>ESR, PT, aPTT</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"AfroBrazilian\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 3.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blood_collection_tube_(1_kind).jpg\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"838\" src=\"images/XOh5Ql7gFiaDtTaMJzuY1747225498.png\"\" width=\"200\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Red</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>No anti-coagulant is added.</p>\n<p>Clot <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a095f733351692777180a29940c98a\">activator</span> without gel</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Serum studies (Proteins, enzymes, hormones)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Whispyhistory\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_vacutainer.jpg\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"825\" src=\"images/tuJ3gv31wToYTtfjLBZB1747225498.png\"\" width=\"200\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Yellow </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Clot activator/Gel separator</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Serum studies (Proteins, enzymes, hormones)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<figure class=\"caption_element\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"Whispyhistory\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grey_vacutainer.jpg\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"714\" src=\"images/y8UCUCKVpgOPNuIs84n51747225498.png\"\" width=\"200\"/>\n<figcaption>{{caption_text}}</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Grey</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Sodium Fluoride<strong> </strong><strong>(Option A)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Blood Glucose</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"300\" src=\"images/aIRCTceJ67xetZjBzXGN1747225498.png\"\" width=\"300\"/></p>\n<p><strong>Green </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Heparin<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Option D ruled out)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>ABG, Osmotic fragility, flow cytometry, immunophenotyping</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p><br/><img src=\"images/BnI2TKJUkOahGu7PMxVl1747225498.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/3VRj3LF1hTS34z6LvERe1747225498.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/XOh5Ql7gFiaDtTaMJzuY1747225498.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/tuJ3gv31wToYTtfjLBZB1747225498.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/y8UCUCKVpgOPNuIs84n51747225498.png\"\"/><br/><img src=\"images/aIRCTceJ67xetZjBzXGN1747225498.png\"\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Atlas of Clinical Hematology, Chapter 1, Page 4<br/>Laboratory tube collection</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532720","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":7,"stem":"Which amino acid is necessary for the production of nitric oxide in the blood vessels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Citrulline"},{"label":"B","text":"Arginine"},{"label":"C","text":"Histidine"},{"label":"D","text":"Tryptophan"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Arginine","solutionHtml":"data-dictid=\"e08c004e8f1692777194c9d37e17ad\"&amp;gt;oxide (NO) is a vital signalling molecule involved in <strong>vasodilation</strong> and various other <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6c2c85ae6b16927771950809419a59\">physiological</span> processes. <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5536b77611169277719356d32e638a\">Nitric</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e08c004e8f1692777194c9d37e17ad\">oxide</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3fda45538a1692777199ec2a5478a5\">synthesis</span> in blood vessels requires the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"543f207eb51692777181723491a358\">amino</span> acid <strong>arginine</strong>, which serves as its precursor.\n\n<h4><strong>Steps in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5536b77611169277719356d32e638a\">Nitric</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e08c004e8f1692777194c9d37e17ad\">Oxide</span> Synthesis:</strong></h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Enzyme Involved</strong>:\n\n\t<ul>\n<li><strong>Nitric <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e08c004e8f1692777194c9d37e17ad\">oxide</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d2bd91ccac169277719941254e1795\">synthase</span> (NOS)</strong> converts <strong>L-arginine</strong> to <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5536b77611169277719356d32e638a\">nitric</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e08c004e8f1692777194c9d37e17ad\">oxide</span> (NO) and <strong>L-citrulline</strong> in the presence of oxygen and cofactors like NADPH and tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Physiological Role of NO</strong>:\n\t<ul>\n<li>NO is a potent <strong>vasodilator</strong> that relaxes <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"526ede3fee169277720184378acc5e\">vascular</span> smooth muscle cells, thus regulating <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"263ee5444e1692777183a7e8e704da\">blood pressure</span> and improving blood flow.</li>\n<li>It also has roles in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"66748a30e91692777193358c35b2b0\">neurotransmission</span> and immune response.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Citrulline</strong> <strong>(Option A)</strong> is a byproduct of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5536b77611169277719356d32e638a\">nitric</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e08c004e8f1692777194c9d37e17ad\">oxide</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3fda45538a1692777199ec2a5478a5\">synthesis</span> and can be recycled back into <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e61a190afe16927771828271a46f94\">arginine</span> via the <strong>urea cycle</strong>, but it is not directly used in NO production.</p>\n<p><strong>Histidine</strong> <strong>(Option C)</strong> is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ae13cdc5c3169277719683e9f6bda5\">precursor</span> for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b5d4ddbfe71692777189a12acd5502\">histamine</span> and is involved in immune responses, but it does not play a role in NO synthesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Tryptophan</strong> <strong>(Option D)</strong> is a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ae13cdc5c3169277719683e9f6bda5\">precursor</span> for <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"06eba971ce169277719884f8c62937\">serotonin</span> and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2a026dbfb616927771921ae6ec0c02\">melatonin</span> but is not involved in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5536b77611169277719356d32e638a\">nitric</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e08c004e8f1692777194c9d37e17ad\">oxide</span> production.</p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34251644/</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532722","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":8,"stem":"For testing blood clotting/ coagulation parameters, blood is collected in which color-coded vacutainer tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red"},{"label":"B","text":"Green"},{"label":"C","text":"Blue"},{"label":"D","text":"Gray"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"Blue","solutionHtml":"lass=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"42f1e4deb8169277718412c0681a54\"&amp;gt;coagulation studies, such as<strong> Prothrombin Time (PT) and Activated Partial Thromboplastin Time (aPTT).</strong> The <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"b5fc7729f316927771817f809648f5\">anticoagulant</span> preserves the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"42f1e4deb8169277718412c0681a54\">coagulation</span> factors for accurate testing.\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Colour</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Uses</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Purple/Lavender<strong> </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"74\" src=\"images/exp_94_40_1.jpg\" width=\"254\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">EDTA (Ca2+ chelating anticoagulant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">CBC, Peripheral Smear, ESR, HbA1c</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Light Blue<strong> (Option B) </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"410\" src=\"images/exp_94_40_2.jpg\" width=\"254\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Citrate (Ca2+ chelating anticoagulant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">ESR, PT, aPTT</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Red <strong>(Option A)</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"200\" src=\"images/exp_94_40_3.jpg\" width=\"129\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">No anti-coagulant is added</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Serum studies (Proteins, enzymes, hormones)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Yellow</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"226\" src=\"images/exp_94_40_4.jpg\" width=\"135\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Clot activator/Gel separator</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Serum studies (Proteins, enzymes, hormones)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Grey <strong>(Option D)</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"206\" src=\"images/exp_94_40_5.jpg\" width=\"131\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Sodium Fluoride</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Blood Glucose</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Green <strong>(Option B)</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"202\" src=\"images/exp_94_40_6.jpg\" width=\"192\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Heparin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">ABG, Osmotic fragility, flow cytometry, immunophenotyping</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_40_1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_40_2.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_40_3.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_40_4.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_40_5.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_40_6.jpg\"/><br/><br/>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436532724","topic":"PrepLadder · 85.Previous Year Questions","number":9,"stem":"Basophilic stippling of red blood cells is most characteristically seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron deficiency anaemia X"},{"label":"B","text":"Lead poisoning"},{"label":"C","text":"Megaloblastic anemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary spherocytosis"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Lead poisoning","solutionHtml":"ead toxicity.\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"292\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_1.jpg\" width=\"444\"/></p>\n<p><strong>Acute lead poisoning</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It’s rare, and there may be exacerbations of chronic lead poisoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Astringent or <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"84433cae211692777192a484f539bc\">metallic</span> taste, dry throat, thirst, burning <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1665a40f1016927771801899eaed5d\">abdominal</span> pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"255674f3271692777195c172d86daf\">peripheral</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f3739d892c1692777184b2e134615c\">circulatory</span> collapse, paraesthesia, depression, coma, and death.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:628px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Chronic lead <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"072b8af16e1692777196f21b086f92\">poisoning</span> (also known as Plumbism or Saturnism)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Clinical manifestations </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Description </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Facial pallor</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"222\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_2.jpg\" width=\"306\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>It is caused by <strong>vasospasm </strong>and specifically occurs <strong>circumorally</strong>, which is the <strong>earliest and most specific feature.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Anemia</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"133\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_3.jpg\" width=\"222\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type: Microcytic-hypochromic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Enzymes inhibited: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ferrochelatase</strong></li>\n<li><strong>ALA-dehydratase</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Coproporphyrinogen oxidase</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood picture:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cabot rings in RBCs</strong></li>\n<li>Microcytic-hypochromic</li>\n<li>Sideroblasts (nucleated red cells)</li>\n<li>Anisocytosis</li>\n<li>Poikilocytosis</li>\n<li>Polycythemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Basophilic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"37d5ea489a1692777199ec08c05fe6\">stippling</span> or Punctate basophilia</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"145\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_4.jpg\" width=\"222\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple blue-coloured pinhead dots in RBCs due to the toxic action of lead on <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4a759c9f1016927771964b8325ac2f\">porphyrin</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b1a045f5f169277719210c0ac7a1e\">metabolism</span> <strong>(inhibition of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0f160e4e42169277718771b715516a\">enzyme</span> 5- Pyrimidine nucleotidase) </strong>(PNN).</li>\n<li>Accumulation of ribosomes in RBC</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Lead line or Burtonian lines</strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"157\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_5.jpg\" width=\"222\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>The <strong>stippled blue line </strong>observed on the gums, resulting from a <strong>subepithelial deposit of granules </strong>that develops within one week, is seen in 50-70% of cases and is more common in individuals with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3e0ddc7b5f1692777183d57869aa5a\">carious</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"17dfb015d2169277720023db8fe9e5\">teeth</span> (bacteria <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a1b06f401f1692777203a53393ba98\">form</span> lead sulfide).</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Lead osteopathy/Lead line (Bone-line) </strong></p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"180\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_6.jpg\" width=\"179\"/></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>In children, <strong>calcium deposition</strong> occurs at the <strong>growth plates of bones</strong> (specifically the lower end of the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"512283ec9816927772024c89478b7d\">femur</span> and the upper end of the tibia), which appears as a <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"76584c702f1692777197ee90a74461\">radiopaque</span> band and occurs over 4 weeks due to the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f03ca2d2a716927771901227bc175a\">inhibition</span> of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"fc8c28347a16927771945680bd00be\">osteoclastic</span> activity.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Colic and constipation</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Painter's Colic or Devonshire colic)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Late presentation occurs in 85% of cases, typically at night, and is characterised by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c76bdef91c16927771983b9c48eb89\">spasmodic</span> pain relieved by pressure.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Lead <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"f3e67cebd51692777194f5257898e4\">palsy</span> </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Predominantly motor features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dry belly ache</li>\n<li>Wrist drop <strong>(Radial nerve)</strong></li>\n<li>Foot drop <strong>(Common <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1e6451edc31692777195c54367b1ad\">peroneal</span> nerve)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"157\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_7.jpg\" width=\"222\"/><img alt=\"\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"146\" src=\"images/exp_94_115_8.jpg\" width=\"222\"/></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Encephalopathy </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>This condition is seen in nearly all cases, is associated with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"e2ea78189716927772003259fe6707\">tetraethyl lead</span> exposure, primarily affects children, results in permanent brain damage in 85% of cases, and accounts for 25% of fatalities.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Reproductive system</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Amenorrhea</li>\n<li>Dysmenorrhea</li>\n<li>Abortion</li>\n<li>Sterility derangements (both sexes)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Cardiovascular system and Kidneys</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension (vascular constriction)</li>\n<li>Permanent arteriolar degeneration</li>\n<li>Nephritis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Gout</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Saturnine gout</strong> (due to decreased <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0f7653c9ef1692777187deaa35f749\">excretion</span> of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ffa20262931692777201caef35de16\">uric</span> acid)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Others </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>These include generalised weakness, anorexia, dyspepsia, emaciation, exhaustion, irritability, foul breath, headache, vertigo, alopecia, and dizziness.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:602px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Conditions associated with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0bc69d6cf8169277718209818c7522\">Basophilic</span> stippling:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lead poisoning</strong></li>\n<li>Thalassemia (Beta-thalassemia)</li>\n<li><strong>Sideroblastic anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Arsenic poisoning</li>\n<li>Alcoholism</li>\n<li>Pyrimidine 5′-Nucleotidase deficiency</li>\n<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Severe infections (e.g., malaria)</li>\n<li>Myelodysplastic syndromes</li>\n<li>Chronic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9d21dd5d3116927771907405b181bc\">inflammatory</span> conditions</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Iron <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"209b85faf71692777185767d43e3bd\">deficiency</span> anaemia (Option A):</strong> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d09dc1da551692777192706b7f882e\">microcytic</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"07ccd8f461169277718970a6f1a131\">hypochromic</span> cells, but no <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0bc69d6cf8169277718209818c7522\">basophilic</span> stippling.</p>\n<p><strong>Megaloblastic anaemia (Option C)</strong>: <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"04173aa3b71692777191ff88525398\">macrocytosis</span> with hypersegmented neutrophils, no stippling.</p>\n<p><strong>Hereditary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"d30f4533661692777199385959bd9f\">spherocytosis</span> (Option D):</strong> spherocytes, increased MCHC, but no stippling.</p><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_1.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_2.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_3.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_4.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_5.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_6.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_7.jpg\"/><br/><img src=\"images/exp_94_115_8.jpg\"/><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">.</div>","tags":""}]}